    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 4\n    nc = 11\n    facility_coords = [(38, 62), (16, 81), (58, 39), (34, 66)]\n    customer_coords = [(75, 64), (31, 16), (50, 3), (89, 80), (35, 70), (39, 42), (42, 68), (56, 46), (50, 9), (21, 68), (24, 38)]\n    capacities = [104.0, 121.0, 88.0, 186.0]\n    demands = [18.0, 23.0, 43.0, 20.0, 24.0, 46.0, 25.0, 12.0, 35.0, 48.0, 27.0]\n    fixed_costs = [243.0, 458.0, 171.0, 378.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: at most 3 facilities open\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 3)\n\n    # Policy: no facility serves > 52% of total demand\n    total_demand = 321.0\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= 0.52*total_demand)\n\n    # Policy: F0 requires F1\n    m.addConstr(open_f[0] <= open_f[1])\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Distribution Network Decision \u2013 Facility Activation and Shipment Plan\n\nOverview\nThe company is evaluating its distribution network and must decide which candidate warehouses to place into operation and how to route shipments from those locations to the customer base. The objective is to meet all customer demand while minimizing total landed cost, which includes both the fixed cost of activating facilities and the variable cost of moving product based on distance and shipped volume.\n\nDecision Required\nWe will identify the combination of distribution centers to open and the shipment plan from opened facilities to each customer such that:\n- Every customer\u2019s demand is fulfilled in full.\n- Each facility\u2019s outbound volume stays within its stated capacity.\n- Shipments originate only from facilities that have been activated.\n- Total cost (facility activation plus transportation) is as low as possible within the company\u2019s operating policies.\n\nBusiness Requirements and Operating Policies\n- Customer service: All customer demand must be served in full. If it is cost-effective and consistent with resiliency policies, a single customer may be supplied by more than one operating distribution center.\n- Facility activation: A facility may ship only if it is opened. Opening any facility incurs a fixed operating cost for the planning horizon.\n- Capacity discipline: No facility may ship more than its available capacity.\n- Transportation costing: Variable shipping cost is calculated from the company\u2019s transport rate applied to the distance between a facility and a customer and the volume shipped. Distances will be measured consistently from the map coordinates provided in the Annex.\n- Network governance limit: To align with management oversight capacity, the company will operate at most 3 distribution centers at any given time.\n- Resiliency against concentration risk: To avoid over-reliance on any single location, no distribution center may fulfill more than 52% of the total system demand. This share cap is evaluated against the total demand across all customers and applies to the sum of shipments dispatched from each operating facility.\n- Hub dependency: The facility labeled F0 relies on the regional hub labeled F1. If F0 is activated, F1 must also be activated. This dependency ensures the required hub support structure is in place. (Refer to the Annex for the corresponding location details.)\n\nCosting Approach\n- Fixed costs: Each opened facility adds its fixed operating cost to the total.\n- Variable costs: Transportation cost scales with both distance and the volume shipped, using the company\u2019s transport rate. The rate and all location coordinates are listed in the Annex; distances will be derived from those coordinates in a consistent manner.\n\nDeliverables\n- Recommended set of facilities to open that respects the limit on total operating sites and the F0\u2013F1 dependency.\n- Shipment plan from each opened facility to each customer that:\n  - Fully satisfies all customer demand.\n  - Observes all facility capacities and the 52% share-of-demand policy.\n  - Minimizes total cost (fixed plus transportation).\n\nAssumptions and Clarifications\n- The planning horizon assumes stable demand and capacities as provided in the Annex.\n- Customers may be allocated to one or more operating facilities, provided all policies and capacities are respected.\n- If the network governance limit, share-of-demand cap, and F0\u2013F1 dependency create ties among multiple feasible options with similar economics, the recommendation will include secondary considerations such as service resilience and capacity headroom.\n\nAnnex: Data Inputs\n- Facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (38, 62)\n  - F1: (16, 81)\n  - F2: (58, 39)\n  - F3: (34, 66)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (75, 64)\n  - C1: (31, 16)\n  - C2: (50, 3)\n  - C3: (89, 80)\n  - C4: (35, 70)\n  - C5: (39, 42)\n  - C6: (42, 68)\n  - C7: (56, 46)\n  - C8: (50, 9)\n  - C9: (21, 68)\n  - C10: (24, 38)\n- Facility capacities: [104.0, 121.0, 88.0, 186.0]\n- Customer demands: [18.0, 23.0, 43.0, 20.0, 24.0, 46.0, 25.0, 12.0, 35.0, 48.0, 27.0]\n- Fixed opening costs: [243.0, 458.0, 171.0, 378.0]\n- Transport rate: 0.5",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Distribution Network Decision \u2013 Facility Activation and Shipment Plan\n\nOverview\nThe company is evaluating its distribution network and must decide which candidate warehouses to place into operation and how to route shipments from those locations to the customer base. The objective is to meet all customer demand while minimizing total landed cost, which includes both the fixed cost of activating facilities and the variable cost of moving product based on distance and shipped volume.\n\nDecision Required\nWe will identify the combination of distribution centers to open and the shipment plan from opened facilities to each customer such that:\n- Every customer\u2019s demand is fulfilled in full.\n- Each facility\u2019s outbound volume stays within its stated capacity.\n- Shipments originate only from facilities that have been activated.\n- Total cost (facility activation plus transportation) is as low as possible within the company\u2019s operating policies.\n\nBusiness Requirements and Operating Policies\n- Customer service: All customer demand must be served in full. If it is cost-effective and consistent with resiliency policies, a single customer may be supplied by more than one operating distribution center.\n- Facility activation: A facility may ship only if it is opened. Opening any facility incurs a fixed operating cost for the planning horizon.\n- Capacity discipline: No facility may ship more than its available capacity.\n- Transportation costing: Variable shipping cost is calculated from the company\u2019s transport rate applied to the distance between a facility and a customer and the volume shipped. Distances will be measured consistently from the map coordinates provided in the Annex.\n- Network governance limit: To align with management oversight capacity, the company will operate at most 3 distribution centers at any given time.\n- Resiliency against concentration risk: To avoid over-reliance on any single location, no distribution center may fulfill more than 52% of the total system demand. This share cap is evaluated against the total demand across all customers and applies to the sum of shipments dispatched from each operating facility.\n- Hub dependency: The facility labeled F0 relies on the regional hub labeled F1. If F0 is activated, F1 must also be activated. This dependency ensures the required hub support structure is in place. (Refer to the Annex for the corresponding location details.)\n\nCosting Approach\n- Fixed costs: Each opened facility adds its fixed operating cost to the total.\n- Variable costs: Transportation cost scales with both distance and the volume shipped, using the company\u2019s transport rate. The rate and all location coordinates are listed in the Annex; distances will be derived from those coordinates in a consistent manner.\n\nDeliverables\n- Recommended set of facilities to open that respects the limit on total operating sites and the F0\u2013F1 dependency.\n- Shipment plan from each opened facility to each customer that:\n  - Fully satisfies all customer demand.\n  - Observes all facility capacities and the 52% share-of-demand policy.\n  - Minimizes total cost (fixed plus transportation).\n\nAssumptions and Clarifications\n- The planning horizon assumes stable demand and capacities as provided in the Annex.\n- Customers may be allocated to one or more operating facilities, provided all policies and capacities are respected.\n- If the network governance limit, share-of-demand cap, and F0\u2013F1 dependency create ties among multiple feasible options with similar economics, the recommendation will include secondary considerations such as service resilience and capacity headroom.\n\nAnnex: Data Inputs\n- Facility coordinates: {FACILITY_COORDINATES}\n- Customer coordinates: {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}\n- Facility capacities: {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}\n- Customer demands: {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}\n- Fixed opening costs: {FIXED_COSTS}\n- Transport rate: {TRANSPORT_RATE}"
